<h1>When AI campaign variants drift from the approved master</h1>
<p class="credit">Responses for Shantanu David, exchange4media<br>Ravi Dugal, President, Flipick Pvt. Ltd.</p>

<!-- ============ QUESTION 1 ============ -->
<p class="question">Q1. Can you share a concrete example where AI-generated campaign variants caused meaningful product or visual drift from the approved master?</p>

<p class="lead">In our tests, regenerating a product shot for a new format changed the product itself. With a car, a single eight-second shot turned the approved blue-teal vehicle silver-grey, even in the same studio setting as the reference and with the elements locked and seeded. Across multiple shots, the drift grows. With a tea tin, regenerating the same scene at a different aspect ratio changed the tin's shape, its lid, its stripes and its colour, and the clip came back two seconds shorter.</p>

<p>The fix is restraint rather than a bigger model: build from real, approved images, adapt formats by cropping rather than regenerating, and keep logos and text in an overlay layer so the product is never redrawn.</p>

<h3>Example 1: colour drift on a car</h3>

<p>We gave a generative video model one approved reference image of the vehicle and asked for a single eight-second shot. We then built a second video from real images of the same car, using motion and transitions instead of generation.</p>

<figure>
  <div class="row">
    <img src="/individual/e4m/images/1.jpg" alt="Approved reference image of the car">
    <img src="/individual/e4m/images/2.jpg" alt="Still from generative video at 7 seconds">
    <img src="/individual/e4m/images/3.png" alt="Still from video built from real images">
  </div>
  <figcaption>Left to right: the approved reference, a still from the generative video at seven seconds, and a still from the video built from real images. All three show the same studio and the same angle. The approved car is blue-teal. In the generative video it has turned silver-grey. In the version built from real images it stays blue-teal.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Because the generative still is in the same studio as the reference, the lighting can't explain the change: the model repainted the car. The drift isn't limited to one frame, either. Earlier in the same eight-second clip, at four seconds, the car is on a desert road at sunset, and it is silver there too.</p>

<figure>
  <img class="full" src="/individual/e4m/images/4.png" alt="Still from generative clip at 4 seconds — silver car on desert road">
  <figcaption>The same generative clip at four seconds: a different setting, and the same silver car.</figcaption>
</figure>

<figure>
  <div class="video-pair">
    <div>
      <p class="video-label">Generative video (Aris drift)</p>
      <div class="video-wrap">
        <video src="/individual/e4m/images/5-aris-drift.mp4" preload="metadata" loop muted></video>
        <div class="play-icon"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="video-label">Built from real images (Aris with image transition)</p>
      <div class="video-wrap">
        <video src="/individual/e4m/images/6-aris-image-transition.mp4" preload="metadata" loop muted></video>
        <div class="play-icon"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</figure>

<p>The customer would see a silver car in the generative version, while the one on sale is blue-teal. Building from real, approved images fixes this: the car in every frame is the approved car.</p>

<h3>Example 2: one prompt, two sizes, two different products</h3>

<p>We used the same prompt and the same engine to generate a tea tin scene twice, changing only the requested aspect ratio. We then cropped the first version to the second size instead of regenerating it.</p>

<figure>
  <div class="video-trio">
    <div>
      <p class="video-label">Generated at 16:9</p>
      <div class="video-wrap">
        <video src="/individual/e4m/images/7-teatin-generated-169.mp4" preload="auto" loop></video>
        <div class="play-icon"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="video-label">Generated at 5:4</p>
      <div class="video-wrap">
        <video src="/individual/e4m/images/8-teatin-generated-54.mp4" preload="auto" loop></video>
        <div class="play-icon"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="video-label">Cropped to 5:4 from the 16:9 version</p>
      <div class="video-wrap">
