<p>What changed when the scene was regenerated:</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Element</th><th>Generated at 16:9</th><th>Generated at 5:4</th><th>Type of drift</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Tin shape</td><td>Short, wide cylinder</td><td>Tall, slim cylinder, nearly twice as tall as it is wide</td><td>Product</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Lid</td><td>Low dome with a rounded top</td><td>Taller lid with a raised rim</td><td>Product</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Gold stripes</td><td>Closely spaced, about six</td><td>Wider spacing, about five</td><td>Product</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Colour</td><td>Deep teal</td><td>Lighter, brighter teal-blue</td><td>Product</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Framing</td><td>Tin at mid-distance, props visible</td><td>Closer and lower, tin fills the frame</td><td>Composition</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Props and background</td><td>Spoon and tea leaves beside the tin; soft shelves</td><td>Leaves almost gone; shelves full of jars</td><td>Scene</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Length</td><td>10 seconds</td><td>8 seconds</td><td>Timing</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>The cropped 5:4 version has none of these changes: same tin, same colour, same length.</p>

<h3>Logos and text</h3>

<p>Logos and on-screen text are where generative rendering is weakest: letters soften, numbers change, brand marks distort. Our approach is to keep every logo, price, offer and line of copy in an overlay layer (supers) above the video. The overlay is identical in every size and language, and a price or offer can be changed without touching the footage. The car video above uses this approach.</p>

<figure>
  <img class="full" src="/individual/e4m/images/10-image-overlay.png" alt="Still from car video showing overlay text and logo">
  <figcaption>Logo and copy carried in the overlay layer, not generated into the scene.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>Voice-over timing across languages</h3>

<p>The same script runs to different lengths in different languages, and even in one language the voice may sound rushed when squeezed into a fixed slot. Across hundreds of adaptations, these problems slip through. AI can translate the script and iterate with a text-to-speech engine until the timing fits, but every version still needs an automated quality check.</p>

<!-- ============ QUESTION 2 ============ -->
<p class="question">Q2. In practice, when one campaign becomes dozens of versions, how are those variants currently reviewed and approved? And, in your experience, where does that process tend to break?</p>

<p class="lead">A campaign multiplies quickly. Each video has several shots, and each shot has its own voice, overlays and music, in every language and every size. Checking each point and going back for corrections by hand is very hard. Review breaks down because the work runs through disconnected tools: prompts, script, storyboard, assets, voice-over, translation, text-to-speech and final rendering each happen separately, so nobody sees the whole picture.</p>

<p>When those steps run in a single pipeline, checks can happen automatically: before rendering, to confirm that scripts fit their time slots and every asset is in place, and after rendering, to check every shot of every version. Most problems can be flagged, and many fixed, before a person ever reviews the output.</p>

<p>In one recent retail campaign, 12 versions across three languages (English, Hindi and Marathi) and four sizes, our automated audio check found that:</p>

<ul>
  <li>every English version failed on all four shots, because the audio was cut off and kept only 43% to 69% of the script's words;</li>
  <li>each Hindi version had one failing shot, where the speech was audibly rushed at more than 2.3 words per second;</li>
  <li>every Marathi version passed.</li>
</ul>

<p>A person reviewing dozens of finished files could easily miss problems like these, especially in a language they don't speak.</p>

<figure>
  <img class="full" src="/individual/e4m/images/11-audio-qc.png" alt="Automated audio QC report across 12 campaign versions">
  <figcaption>Automated audio check across 12 versions of one campaign. Red marks a failing shot.</figcaption>
</figure>

